    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты же её уволила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You fired her.' Notice the word order: 'you' comes first, and 'to fire' finishes the sentence. Then Kristina replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Она сама уволилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She quit herself.' The first content word means 'it', and 'quit' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая разница?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What difference does it make?' You'll hear 'what' all the time in the show, here connected with 'difference'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я предлагаю сделку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I propose a deal.' Notice the word order: 'I' comes first, and 'deal' finishes the sentence. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Какую?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind?' The key word here means 'what', which Kristina combines with 'what'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я буду делать суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will make sushi.' The first content word means 'I', and 'sushi' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты вернёшь Вику.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And you will bring Vika back.' Chef Viktor puts 'you' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'will get back'. Kristina answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Это смешно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is funny.' In Russian, 'this' leads into 'ridiculous' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Гости ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The customers are waiting.' Chef Viktor starts with 'visitors' and pairs it with 'are waiting' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine.' Kristina puts 'good' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я согласна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I agree.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'agree' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is excellent.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'perfect' straight to 'perfect'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я начинаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am starting.' You'll hear 'I' all the time in the show, here connected with 'am starting'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
